1. Answer B, "two"

Normally if I write an array like [1, "two", 3], TypeScript just says it is an array with numbers and strings in it. So arr[1] would be string | number, which is option C.

But here we added as const. That locks the array so nothing in it can be changed. Because nothing can change, TypeScript knows exactly what is sitting at each spot. Spot 1 has "two" and it will always be "two". So the type is "two" itself, not just string. This is the literal type idea from the doc.

