2. Answer B, error saying y does not exist on C

My first guess was that if the interface has y?, then the class would get a y too. But that is not what implements does.

implements only checks that my class has everything the interface needs. Since y is optional, my class does not need it, so the check passes. But TypeScript does not add y to my class either. The class only has x, because x is all I wrote. So c.y = 10 fails because there really is no y on C.

The doc has almost this exact example in the Implements section.

