4. Answer C, never

The & means the type has to be both { id: number } and { id: string } at the same time. So id would have to be a number and a string together. That is impossible.

When something is impossible, TypeScript uses never. So A["id"] is never.

The part that surprised me is that writing the type does not give an error by itself, which is why D is wrong. TypeScript is happy to let me declare it. The problem only shows up later when I try to actually give id a value, because no value can ever fit.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
5. Answer B, not all paths return a value

The switch only handles circle and square. There is no case for triangle.

I expected TypeScript to warn me about the missing case, but it does not do that on its own. That is why A and D are wrong.

The error is there for a different reason. The function says it returns number. But if the shape is a triangle, neither case matches, the switch does nothing, and the function ends without returning anything. So TypeScript says the function can finish without giving back a number.

If I want TypeScript to actually catch the missing case, I have to add the default with never, like the doc shows. That is the same trick I used in employee.factory.ts in my code.
